STAAD PLANE  COVERED ENTRY STAIRWELL - TRANSVERSE FRAME, 1 m STRIP
*
* ==========================================================================
*  COVERED ENTRY STAIRWELL - UNDERGROUND CBRN SHELTER, PUNE   REV F
*
*  WHY THIS STRUCTURE EXISTS
*  The Rev E approach was an OPEN cut 4.800 x 1.500, 2.000 deep, with the
*  outer door at its foot.  Three faults, all fixed by roofing it:
*    (a) no gravity outfall - every drop of rain had to be pumped, so a
*        pump failure flooded the only entrance;
*    (b) the entrance sat at the bottom of a permanent sump, which is where
*        dense CBRN agents collect - the worst possible place for the door
*        of a CBRN shelter;
*    (c) it could not simply be lidded at grade: the headroom at the top of
*        the steps would have been ZERO.
*  Rev F roofs the stair and moves the external door to GRADE at the head
*  of the stair, with the ground falling away from it.
*
*  THIS MODEL is the transverse box frame at the platform end, where the
*  walls retain the most.  A 1 m strip, PLANE frame.  The stair, the
*  headhouse and the shelter below are NOT in this model and do not change.
*
*  clear width 1.500   walls 250   floor 250   roof 250
*  floor -2.000   roof soffit +0.400   berm over the roof +0.950
*  wall span (c/c) 2.650   roof span (c/c) 1.750
*  M30 / Fe500,  Ec = 27.386 x 10^6 kN/m2
*
*  SOIL
*   engineered fill  gamma = 18.0 kN/m3,  phi = 30 deg
*   at rest  K0 = 1 - sin(phi) = 0.50   (propped wall, so at-rest and
*   not active - the roof props the head and the floor props the foot)
*   construction surcharge 10.0 kPa -> K0 x q = 5.0 kPa
*   earth over the roof 0.300 m -> 5.40 kN/m2
*   subgrade modulus 100000 kN/m3, as adopted for the shelter mat
*
*  Units: metre, kilonewton.  X across the stairwell, Y up.
* ==========================================================================
*
START JOB INFORMATION
ENGINEER DATE 25-Aug-2026
JOB NAME ENTRY STAIRWELL - REV F
END JOB INFORMATION
*
INPUT WIDTH 79
UNIT METER KN
JOINT COORDINATES
1 0.0000 -2.1250 0.0;
2 0.2917 -2.1250 0.0;
3 0.5833 -2.1250 0.0;
4 0.8750 -2.1250 0.0;
5 1.1667 -2.1250 0.0;
6 1.4583 -2.1250 0.0;
7 1.7500 -2.1250 0.0;
8 0.0000 -1.4625 0.0;
9 0.0000 -0.8000 0.0;
10 0.0000 -0.1375 0.0;
11 0.0000 0.5250 0.0;
12 1.7500 -1.4625 0.0;
13 1.7500 -0.8000 0.0;
14 1.7500 -0.1375 0.0;
15 1.7500 0.5250 0.0;
16 0.2917 0.5250 0.0;
17 0.5833 0.5250 0.0;
18 0.8750 0.5250 0.0;
19 1.1667 0.5250 0.0;
20 1.4583 0.5250 0.0;
*
MEMBER INCIDENCES
1 1 2;
2 2 3;
3 3 4;
4 4 5;
5 5 6;
6 6 7;
7 1 8;
8 8 9;
9 9 10;
10 10 11;
11 7 12;
12 12 13;
13 13 14;
14 14 15;
15 11 16;
16 16 17;
17 17 18;
18 18 19;
19 19 20;
20 20 15;
*
START GROUP DEFINITION
MEMBER
_FLOOR 1 TO 6
_WALL_S 7 TO 10
_WALL_N 11 TO 14
_ROOF 15 TO 20
END GROUP DEFINITION
*
DEFINE MATERIAL START
ISOTROPIC M30
E 27386128
POISSON 0.2
DENSITY 25.0
ALPHA 1e-05
DAMP 0.05
TYPE CONCRETE
STRENGTH FCU 30000
END DEFINE MATERIAL
*
MEMBER PROPERTY
* 1 m strip: ZD = 1.000, YD = the element thickness
1 TO 6 PRIS YD 0.250 ZD 1.000
7 TO 14 PRIS YD 0.250 ZD 1.000
15 TO 20 PRIS YD 0.250 ZD 1.000
*
CONSTANTS
MATERIAL M30 ALL
*
SUPPORTS
* floor on elastic foundation: a vertical spring at each floor joint,
* k = subgrade x tributary length x 1 m strip.  FX released at one joint
* only, to restrain the frame horizontally without inventing a prop.
2 FIXED BUT FX MZ KFY 29167
3 FIXED BUT FX MZ KFY 29167
4 FIXED BUT FX MZ KFY 29167
5 FIXED BUT FX MZ KFY 29167
6 FIXED BUT FX MZ KFY 29167
7 FIXED BUT FX MZ KFY 14583
* one joint also holds FX - it stands for base friction and passive
* resistance on the floor slab, which are not modelled explicitly
1 FIXED BUT MZ KFY 14583
*
* ============================ LOADING =====================================
LOAD 1 LOADTYPE Dead TITLE DL SELFWEIGHT PLUS EARTH OVER THE ROOF
SELFWEIGHT Y -1.0
MEMBER LOAD
* 0.300 m of berm over the roof = 5.40 kN/m2
15 TO 20 UNI GY -5.400
*
LOAD 2 LOADTYPE Live TITLE LL ON THE BERM OVER THE ROOF
MEMBER LOAD
15 TO 20 UNI GY -1.500
*
LOAD 3 LOADTYPE Soil TITLE EARTH AT REST BOTH WALLS
MEMBER LOAD
* TRAP values computed PER MEMBER - STAAD's TRAP has no datum, so one
* trapezoid per wall would give a sawtooth that the statics check misses.
7 TRAP GX 27.6750 21.7125
8 TRAP GX 21.7125 15.7500
9 TRAP GX 15.7500 9.7875
10 TRAP GX 9.7875 3.8250
11 TRAP GX -27.6750 -21.7125
12 TRAP GX -21.7125 -15.7500
13 TRAP GX -15.7500 -9.7875
14 TRAP GX -9.7875 -3.8250
*
LOAD 4 LOADTYPE Soil TITLE SURCHARGE 10 kPa AT REST BOTH WALLS
MEMBER LOAD
7 TO 10 UNI GX 5.0000
11 TO 14 UNI GX -5.0000
*
LOAD 5 LOADTYPE Soil TITLE EARTH ONE SIDE ONLY - BACKFILLING CASE
MEMBER LOAD
7 TRAP GX 27.6750 21.7125
8 TRAP GX 21.7125 15.7500
9 TRAP GX 15.7500 9.7875
10 TRAP GX 9.7875 3.8250
*
* ---- IS 456 : 2000 Cl 36.4.1 and Table 18 -------------------------------
LOAD COMB 101 1.5 DL + 1.5 LL + 1.5 EARTH + 1.5 SURCHARGE
1 1.5 2 1.5 3 1.5 4 1.5
LOAD COMB 102 1.5 DL + 1.5 EARTH + 1.5 SURCHARGE  NO ROOF LL
1 1.5 3 1.5 4 1.5
LOAD COMB 103 0.9 DL + 1.5 EARTH + 1.5 SURCHARGE  MINIMUM RESTRAINT
1 0.9 3 1.5 4 1.5
LOAD COMB 104 1.5 DL + 1.5 EARTH ONE SIDE  CONSTRUCTION
1 1.5 5 1.5
LOAD COMB 201 1.0 DL + 1.0 LL + 1.0 EARTH + 1.0 SURCHARGE  SERVICE
1 1.0 2 1.0 3 1.0 4 1.0
*
PERFORM ANALYSIS PRINT STATICS CHECK
*
PRINT SUPPORT REACTION ALL
PRINT MEMBER FORCES ALL
FINISH
